@page "/docs/tools/vs-code-accessibility"

@* The audit in the editor (0.9.4: out/audit.js): a front end over izzy audit <path> --run --json. What a run checks,
   the verdicts and the reports are on the Accessibility section's audit pages: this page says where the results show
   in VS Code. Left out on purpose (Nov 26, 2026): 0.9.4's comparison with the last audit, Open Audit Report and Open
   tasks.md. They need the run's files, and the extension passes no --out and reads landed, not written, so with
   this izzy they never show. Add them here and to the table on docs/tools/vs-code when the extension reads written. *@

<ArticlePage Section="tools" Slug="vs-code-accessibility">
    <Lead>
        Check Page Accessibility runs <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit">izzy audit</Anchor> on the page
        you're editing and lists what it found in the side bar and the Problems panel. It reports and changes
        no code.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Check a page</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Right-click a page in the editor or the Explorer and choose Check Page Accessibility, or use the
            icon in the editor's title bar. Check Accessibility, in the Project view under Check, lists every
            page of your app and takes a path you type. For a route with a parameter, such as
            <Code>/orders/{id:int}</Code>, the extension asks for a value and remembers it.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            izzy builds and starts your app, audits the page in Chrome or Edge, and stops the app.
            <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit">Audit a page</Anchor> says what a run does. The audit
            runs the saved files: the extension asks when the page has unsaved changes. When the app doesn't
            build, the message names the error and takes you to it. A component with no <Code>@@page</Code>
            route can't be opened alone: check a page that uses it.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Read the results</Heading>
        <Table Items="Places" Caption="Where an audit's results show" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-prose" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Place" Width="Length.Rem(12)">@context!.Name</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What it shows">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            The view groups findings by the rule that failed, and calls each group a problem. Needs a person
            to check lists each check axe-core couldn't measure, such as contrast over a picture, with how
            many elements. These are neither findings nor passes: look at each one. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/automated-and-manual">Automated and manual checks</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            With a free account, each finding has its <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit-verdicts">verdict</Anchor>
            and the reason for it. After a fix, Check Again on a page audits it again. The results stay in the
            view until you clear them.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            For <Code>report.md</Code>, a task list and a comparison with an earlier run, run
            <Code>izzy audit</Code> in a terminal: see <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit-reports">Reports</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">The whole app</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            With Pro, Whole App, in the list of pages, audits every page your app links to, or its sitemap
            lists, in one run.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">What a clean result means</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            No findings means the automated checks found nothing on that page as it loaded. It doesn't mean
            the page meets WCAG. The audit doesn't
            open closed dialogs or menus, and doesn't check keyboard order or what a screen reader says. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit-checks">What the audit checks</Anchor> and
            <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/testing">Testing your app</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record Row(string Name, string Text);

    private static readonly Row[] Places =
    [
        new("The Accessibility view", "The findings by page, then problem, then element, the most serious first. Copy Selector, on an element, copies its CSS selector."),
        new("The Problems panel", "Each problem on the page's @page line, with a link to how to fix it. A finding on an address no .razor file has a route for isn't listed here."),
        new("A details page", "For each problem: the elements, their markup, the ways to fix it, and the verdict when the run has one."),
    ];
}
